    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c52b0799-b712-5810-93a4-fe16e567bf32",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{72}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{72}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{72}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{72}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{{(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 72\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{648}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{648+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 648+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-216 \\\\ x & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-6,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-6)^{2}}{3}=-12.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-6,-12).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ca193056-cb5a-5c97-bc0e-2cfd6b80198c",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
