      ":question/id": "e7f45431-940a-5145-9ab3-9bece18bfc52",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)+5(y-8)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)+5(y-8)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)+5(y-8)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+5\\cdot 2(y-8) \\\\  & =10(y-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+2}\\,i+10(y-8)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+2}\\,i+10(y-8)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+2}=0 \\\\ 10(y-8)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=8.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,8).$",
