      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{2} ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y) \\\\  & =2x-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y) \\\\  & =24x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)\\,i+24x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)\\,i+24x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)=0 \\\\ 24x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ 72\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=6.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{2} ,$ at two points: $(6,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$",
