    "question": {
      ":question/id": "56a21302-5562-54e5-af55-8093f63759f4",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-x$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =12x+0-1 \\\\  & =12x-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =0+8y-0 \\\\  & =8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=(12x-1)\\,i+8y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(12x-1)\\,i+8y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}12x-1=0 \\\\ 8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\frac{1}{12}.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{12},0)\\,.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4ca2a410-91c2-5422-b06d-2c3d864a90b5",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
