    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e7530768-ad3e-5703-89f9-28e7cef9e55d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{125}{y}-\\frac{1y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{125}{y}-\\frac{1y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{125}{y}-\\frac{1y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{125}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{1y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{125}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{125}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{125}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{125}{{(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ 125\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{125}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{125+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 125+{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-125 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{1}=-25.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-5,-25).$",
