    "question": {
      ":question/id": "245bb266-495c-53ab-a0c1-629efc59e196",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3x$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =10x+0-3 \\\\  & =10x-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3x) \\\\  & =0+2y-0 \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=(10x-3)\\,i+2y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(10x-3)\\,i+2y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-3=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\frac{3}{10}.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(\\frac{3}{10},0)\\,.$",
