    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d1e3e97b-ecb7-5305-8427-453cc6858586",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x) \\\\  & =6x+0-4 \\\\  & =6x-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-4x) \\\\  & =0+4y-0 \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=(6x-4)\\,i+4y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(6x-4)\\,i+4y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-4=0 \\\\ 4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(\\frac{2}{3},0)\\,.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9e5f7b6f-615d-5aa3-a362-15a6caf3b03e",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
