  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124445,
      "id": "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340",
      "title": "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a31061ea-190b-55ff-838c-98d3f127cbe8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $g(x,y)=10xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =10y+0-2x \\\\  & =2(5y-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =10x+2y-0 \\\\  & =2(5x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=2(5y-x)\\,i+2(5x+y)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n2(5y-x)\\,i+2(5x+y)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5y-x=0 \\\\ 5x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=5y.$ Substituting these into the second equation, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(5y)+y & =0 \\\\ 26y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get $x=5\\cdot 0=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,0).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "932feebb-9a2b-5452-98ad-b8301612e598",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
