    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d3ac940e-53f4-5c40-a121-045170a500ca",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{2}{y}-\\frac{4y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{2}{y}-\\frac{4y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{2}{y}-\\frac{4y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{x})j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{x} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot {(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{4}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{32}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{32+4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 32+4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{4}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-2,-1).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a509b888-9e3e-5328-a63f-2db8db9dd1fc",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "79db1063-3cbc-5a89-b2ba-0defeb0782a6",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
