    "question": {
      ":question/id": "12ceeb85-5f64-5049-bbdc-94315cda3bd3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+3(y-6)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+3(y-6)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+5}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+5)+3(y-6)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot 2(y-6) \\\\  & =6(y-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,i+6(y-6)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}\\,i+6(y-6)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+5}=0 \\\\ 6(y-6)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=6.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,6).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "60913c7e-d2bb-5ea0-a88a-acc23c1d8cc3",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
