    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e75b1361-de88-512a-913f-d70f3014c857",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,i+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0 \\\\ 6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ 9\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=3.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ at two points: $(3,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "f543ce10-5d6d-56e3-818a-2b1b56b13e5e",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
